# Declaring a procedure whose name is a builtin is LEGAL (spec §3.7: builtins
# lex as TK_IDENT, so they stay legal procedure names) -- but it silently takes
# over every unqualified call to that name in the package, and until 2026-08-09
# nothing said so. That is the one papercut on the 1.0 list that produced a
# WRONG ANSWER rather than an error: core:utf8's `decode` called `utf8.len`,
# reached its own `len` instead of the builtin, and recursed until the stack
# guard fired -- which is why that package's API is `count` today.
#
# The program below is valid and runs; the warning is the assertion, and this
# lane captures stderr only -- it never runs the program. That is how the note
# here came to claim the call prints 7: it does NOT. It prints 4, because the
# builtin is what actually gets selected, and no gate could see the difference.
# `tests/shadow_builtin_selection.ty` runs it and locks the real answer; the
# warning's own wording is wrong against docs/spec/01-lexical.md:201-209.
fn len(s: string) -> int:
    return 7

fn four(s: string) -> int:
    return len(s)                # says "the shadow"; the builtin is selected

fn main():
    print(str(four("abcd")))     # 4 -- see the selection fixture
